00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.870
Hello, and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan, and today we're

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.599
going to be learning about how to
calculate theoretical n percent yield.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.070
Now, when is this really important?

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:15.380
Well, typically it has to do with
stoichiometry and relating to your work

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.760
that you do within the laboratory setting.

00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:22.839
Percent yield is equal to the actual
yield, which is how much you obtain

00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:27.209
within an experiment, so it's also
commonly called the experimental yield,

00:00:27.749 --> 00:00:30.119
divided by the theoretical yield.

00:00:30.435 --> 00:00:34.805
which is how much you obtain when
you calculate it using stoichiometry.

00:00:35.144 --> 00:00:39.405
So just using your calculators and going
through all the mathematical steps of

00:00:39.665 --> 00:00:43.525
what you've been learning about in general
chemistry, and we times it by 100%.

00:00:44.065 --> 00:00:46.904
Now, why is it that we
try to calculate this?

00:00:46.944 --> 00:00:47.974
Let's take a look at it.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:52.835
Now, throughout stoichiometry, we
make the assumption that all reactants

00:00:53.245 --> 00:00:55.815
100 percent convert to products.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.200
meaning that we are assuming
that we are perfect.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.990
The thing is, as we all
know, we are not perfect.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.880
We make errors in the lab.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.860
It is not possible to get
100 percent in chemistry.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.250
You always are making errors all
over the place, which is normal.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.210
You make errors because you're learning.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.640
You're trying to get
your lab skills better.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.630
Sometimes reactions don't completely
occur the way you want them to.

00:01:20.025 --> 00:01:22.575
But there are errors and these are
things that we have to deal with.

00:01:22.635 --> 00:01:24.755
Now, where can these errors come up from?

00:01:25.115 --> 00:01:27.895
Well, sometimes they can come
from maybe just transferring

00:01:27.905 --> 00:01:29.515
chemicals between glassware.

00:01:29.775 --> 00:01:33.525
It's really easy to see like, Hey,
some, some solid got left here.

00:01:33.775 --> 00:01:36.565
Maybe when you're mixing, you
see it on the stirring rod or on

00:01:36.565 --> 00:01:38.765
the spatulas or on the beakers.

00:01:39.214 --> 00:01:43.645
Uh, it's very common the reaction
doesn't go to completion or maybe

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:45.175
you didn't wait long enough.

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:48.865
Like, okay, the lab manual said 15
minutes, but hey, your lab partner

00:01:48.865 --> 00:01:49.914
wanted to leave a little early.

00:01:49.914 --> 00:01:51.395
So you just did it for five minutes.

00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:53.004
So maybe there was an error.

00:01:53.565 --> 00:01:56.674
Other things that could be made
is maybe you made a side product,

00:01:56.674 --> 00:02:00.374
meaning that another reaction
occurred without your knowledge.

00:02:00.845 --> 00:02:05.985
Or the reactant isn't P or let's say the
reactants weren't even pure to begin with.

00:02:06.305 --> 00:02:10.225
So let's say if you just had like some,
maybe not the best quality ingredients.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.130
Well, then, yeah, you're going
to have different, different, uh,

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:17.100
read it products in the very end
or just human error to begin with.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.000
Maybe you just made a mistake or
forgot to do something or added

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.120
too much water or whatever it
is, there are going to be errors.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.400
So when we are calculating this.

00:02:27.005 --> 00:02:30.745
We calculate on paper the theoretical
yield, which is something that

00:02:30.755 --> 00:02:31.945
you've been doing all along.

00:02:31.955 --> 00:02:34.105
This is all stoichiometry calculations.

00:02:34.115 --> 00:02:38.435
So whether it's mass to mass, gas
stoichiometry, solution stoichiometry,

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:41.225
you're assuming that you have
a perfect experiment, right?

00:02:41.225 --> 00:02:45.265
This is all about mass
conservation and being Perfect.

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:47.195
And that is a true law.

00:02:47.245 --> 00:02:51.285
However, we do make errors and
there is mass that can be lost.

00:02:51.805 --> 00:02:55.674
So if something is lost, this
is what the actual yield is

00:02:55.715 --> 00:02:57.114
and what we measure in lab.

00:02:57.484 --> 00:03:01.295
So we compare the two numbers
to see, well, how good did you

00:03:01.295 --> 00:03:02.484
do when you were in the lab?

00:03:02.485 --> 00:03:06.515
It gives us a reference point
to see, can we improve, right?

00:03:06.515 --> 00:03:10.524
One of the things about school is all
about improvement and getting better.

00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:11.575
better, right?

00:03:11.905 --> 00:03:14.744
If you were getting 100 percent on
everything going throughout the gate, then

00:03:14.755 --> 00:03:16.095
you don't need to be in school, right?

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:18.625
You can just start working in
the field that you want to be in.

00:03:19.014 --> 00:03:20.785
But we do this because we want to learn.

00:03:20.825 --> 00:03:22.205
We want to grow.

00:03:22.694 --> 00:03:26.285
And the whole idea here is when it comes
to chemistry is products are always lost.

00:03:26.285 --> 00:03:30.715
We're always trying to search for them
with our little magnifying glass, but

00:03:30.744 --> 00:03:32.575
we always are losing a little bit.

00:03:32.575 --> 00:03:36.394
So we're going to be using this formula
to help us try to understand this.

00:03:36.855 --> 00:03:40.915
So let's try to take a look at an example
of how this could work and then we'll get

00:03:40.915 --> 00:03:42.945
into some harder stoichiometry examples.

00:03:48.975 --> 00:03:54.875
In the reaction box, there are 6 moles of
hydrogen gas and 3 moles of nitrogen gas.

00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:59.034
Based on the reaction, determine
the theoretical yield based on

00:03:59.035 --> 00:04:00.575
the following chemical expression.

00:04:01.245 --> 00:04:04.684
Well, if we were doing this and we
wanted to figure out exactly how

00:04:04.684 --> 00:04:08.034
much ammonia you were going to make,
which is your theoretical yield.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.510
What would we do?

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:14.040
Well, the first thing is that we
saw is that we had 6 moles of H2.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.589
So if we wanted to convert that using
this formula, well, we would just use

00:04:18.599 --> 00:04:25.190
our molar coefficients, and we can see
that, okay, for every 3 We are going

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.690
to be making two moles of ammonia.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:31.660
So if we were to do this little
conversion here, we are going to

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.230
quickly see that we would obtain four
moles of ammonia that are possible.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.079
Now, the thing is that this is a
limiting reagent problem because you

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:42.489
were given two different starting points.

00:04:42.979 --> 00:04:46.020
We were also told, hey, we have
three moles of nitrogen in that

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.350
box, too, and we can see it as well.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.180
There are these little, the little,
uh, blue orbs that are here.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.240
Um, are reacting as well.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.180
So if I have three moles of nitrogen
and from our chemical equation, we can

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:04.880
see that one mole of nitrogen reacts
and it creates two moles of ammonia.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.130
Well, this is going to make a
total of six moles of ammonia.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.389
Well, which one is the theoretical yield?

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:16.520
Well, it's all about which
made less out of the two.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:22.150
So our answer in this case is going to be
that hydrogen was the limiting reagent.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:27.910
And our possible theoretical
yield is going to be, uh, going

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:34.390
to be hydrogen in this case
making only four moles of ammonia.

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:34.899
Okay?

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.430
So our theoretical yield is four.

00:05:38.145 --> 00:05:42.395
Now, if we wanted to calculate,
let's say, percent yield, how

00:05:42.395 --> 00:05:43.805
exactly would you do this?

00:05:44.365 --> 00:05:49.295
Well, so let's say if, however, you
actually only created 3 moles of ammonia

00:05:49.414 --> 00:05:52.505
in the lab, what is your percent yield?

00:05:52.844 --> 00:05:57.395
Well, so this is your actual,
which is 3, your theoretical was 4.

00:05:57.865 --> 00:06:02.110
So what do you got to do is you'll take
3 moles, which is your actual yield.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.480
Divide that by 4 moles,
which was our theoretical.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:06.890
We calculated that together.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:15.660
So telling us that you have about a 75
percent yield within your reaction, which

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.550
is pretty good actually in chemistry
to get about a 75 percent yield.

00:06:18.935 --> 00:06:22.135
A lot of times when you're doing organic
chemistry, you might be lucky if you

00:06:22.135 --> 00:06:26.715
even get 5 or 10 percent yield depending
on what reaction you were trying to do.

00:06:26.855 --> 00:06:27.975
So that's the basic idea.

00:06:28.245 --> 00:06:32.235
How much were you supposed to
make and how much did you actually

00:06:32.305 --> 00:06:33.705
make when you did the experiment?

00:06:34.155 --> 00:06:34.445
All right.

00:06:34.575 --> 00:06:37.445
Let's take a look at some
different stoichiometry examples.

00:06:38.155 --> 00:06:41.395
from the air to form lithium bicarbonate.

00:06:42.095 --> 00:06:46.305
So lithium hydroxide plus CO2
makes lithium bicarbonate or

00:06:46.325 --> 00:06:47.705
lithium hydrogen carbonate.

00:06:48.335 --> 00:06:54.245
Uh, what is the percent yield of lithium
bicarbonate for the reaction if 50

00:06:54.255 --> 00:06:58.225
grams of lithium hydroxide gives 72.

00:06:58.295 --> 00:07:00.325
8 grams of lithium bicarbonate?

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:05.090
The first thing is that what a lot of
students will do is they want to just

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:10.460
divide these two numbers and then times
it by 100 percent and think they are done.

00:07:10.979 --> 00:07:12.940
The thing is that would
be very, very wrong.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.319
We can't just take 50
grams and divide it by 72.

00:07:15.359 --> 00:07:18.089
8 and be done with the problem.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:22.560
We need to convert it to be
the same molecule when we are

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:23.930
comparing the two numbers.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:28.110
So we need to do the stoichiometry
for 50 grams of lithium hydroxide.

00:07:28.539 --> 00:07:31.190
and then convert it to
lithium bicarbonate.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:34.860
So in this case, using mass
stoichiometry to do the conversion.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:39.589
So now whenever we do these, we
always need to check if, if the

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.350
chemical equation is balanced.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:46.599
Typically the easiest way to know if it
is, is if you see coefficients, but with

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.430
this one, there are no coefficients,
but you can, you might be able to

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:51.670
quickly identify that it is balanced.

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:55.370
You have one lithium on each side,
one carbon, and then three oxygens.

00:07:55.739 --> 00:07:58.909
So it's pretty quick to see that,
that this one is balanced, so we

00:07:58.909 --> 00:08:00.130
don't have to do anything else.

00:08:00.669 --> 00:08:04.640
So, what we are going to do is we need
to calculate what the theoretical yield

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.010
is using stoichiometry conversions.

00:08:07.299 --> 00:08:10.240
So we want to write down all the
important pieces of information that

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:15.120
we were given, which was 50 grams
of lithium hydroxide, and then what

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:18.510
our actual yield was, which was 72.

00:08:18.559 --> 00:08:18.909
8.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.645
Keep in mind, whenever there's
actual yields involved, It's always

00:08:23.645 --> 00:08:27.895
generally given to you in some form
or it's what you did in the lab.

00:08:27.905 --> 00:08:31.265
It has to either be given to
you or you did it yourself.

00:08:31.705 --> 00:08:34.704
There's no, you generally
can't calculate it unless you

00:08:34.704 --> 00:08:36.194
were given the percent yield.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:39.760
Um, you have to, usually
something has to be given to you.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:46.419
So if we were to do this, we have 50
grams of lithium hydroxide, so LiOH

00:08:48.339 --> 00:08:55.025
at our disposal, and we are going to
convert this to lithium bicarbonate.

00:08:55.035 --> 00:08:58.415
So what is our first step to do that?

00:08:58.895 --> 00:09:03.255
Well, whenever we are given a
mass, our goal is always the mole.

00:09:03.795 --> 00:09:09.054
So we need to use the molar mass
of lithium hydroxide to get there.

00:09:09.064 --> 00:09:13.684
So what we'll do is we'll use our
periodic table and you'll get 23.

00:09:13.755 --> 00:09:18.225
938 grams A lithium hydroxide,
and if you're watching this, I

00:09:18.225 --> 00:09:22.015
recommend you check that so you can
practice it, and that's equivalent

00:09:22.025 --> 00:09:25.015
to one mole of lithium hydroxide.

00:09:26.105 --> 00:09:29.915
Now from there, what we're going to
do is use our chemical equation, which

00:09:29.915 --> 00:09:36.324
we can see it's a one to one ratio,
so one mole of LiOH is equivalent

00:09:36.345 --> 00:09:41.505
to one mole of LiHCO3, alright?

00:09:41.515 --> 00:09:43.165
So that's a one to one ratio.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.600
And let's change the hydroxide
to match the formula too.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:51.530
Now from there, we're now going
to use the molar mass of lithium

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:54.250
hydroxide to cancel out the last part.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:57.429
But before we do that, let's just
show our units are all canceling,

00:09:57.429 --> 00:10:02.689
so grams cancelled grams, and
then moles cancelled moles.

00:10:03.649 --> 00:10:07.200
Alright, so our molar mass of
lithium hydroxide is going to

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:11.180
be one mole is equivalent to 67.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.830
956 grams.

00:10:14.205 --> 00:10:19.385
And we can see that, hey, these canceled
as well, so one mole cancels one mole.

00:10:20.085 --> 00:10:22.694
And what we will get in the end is 141.

00:10:22.725 --> 00:10:27.955
94 grams of LiHCO3.

00:10:28.345 --> 00:10:32.864
So this is your theoretical yield that
we are here and we have labeled in pink.

00:10:33.975 --> 00:10:35.005
What do we got to do now?

00:10:35.025 --> 00:10:36.625
How do we figure out the percent yield?

00:10:36.785 --> 00:10:39.515
Well, the whole entire thing with
the percent yield is we got to kind

00:10:39.515 --> 00:10:41.515
of plug it into the formula below.

00:10:42.085 --> 00:10:44.354
So we were given 72.

00:10:44.445 --> 00:10:46.615
8 grams is how much we started with.

00:10:47.235 --> 00:10:50.964
So what we are going to do is
essentially take that value and

00:10:50.964 --> 00:10:55.350
then divide it by our 141 0.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:11:02.130
94 grams and we're going to times that
by 100 percent and what we are going to

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:05.840
end up having in the end is about 51.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:09.950
3 percent is our yield in this problem.

00:11:10.519 --> 00:11:12.570
So which isn't, which is pretty good.

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:15.720
I know it sounds like a really low number,
but in terms of percent yields, this is

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.800
pretty good for most chemical reactions.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:21.190
You always want to get higher, but
it's not bad for a chemical reaction.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:23.510
Let's look at another example.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:26.730
So we want to find the
actual yield in this problem.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:30.790
So we're going to be working
backwards, meaning that aluminum

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:36.410
reacts with oxygen to produce aluminum
oxide via the following reaction.

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:42.710
So we have four aluminum reacting with
three oxygen to make two aluminum oxide.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:46.100
So what we are going to do is we're
going to calculate the mass of

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:52.980
aluminum that can be produced if the
reaction of 50 grams of aluminum with

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.420
excess oxygen has a yield of 75%.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:01.310
First things first is we need to
calculate the theoretical yield

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:04.130
starting with 50 grams of aluminum.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:08.300
So we're going to convert that to
aluminum oxide in this problem.

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:12.099
So we've got to kind of look through what
all the important piece of information is.

00:12:12.099 --> 00:12:13.950
So, right, there's a lot of words here.

00:12:14.395 --> 00:12:18.835
But we only need a few of these more
important terms, which was essentially we

00:12:18.835 --> 00:12:23.735
have 50 grams of aluminum, excess oxygen
means it's not going to run out, and then

00:12:23.735 --> 00:12:26.405
we have 75 percent is what our yield is.

00:12:26.905 --> 00:12:27.944
So we're starting with 50.

00:12:28.314 --> 00:12:31.714
So what we're going to do is calculate
what that theoretical yield is.

00:12:32.175 --> 00:12:37.915
Um, so we're going to start
with 50 grams of aluminum and

00:12:37.925 --> 00:12:39.575
we need to get that to moles.

00:12:39.585 --> 00:12:41.825
So how do we get a mass to moles?

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:45.280
Well, we need to use the
molar mass to do that.

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:49.950
So we're going to use our periodic
table to see that, okay, 26.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:55.080
98 grams of aluminum is equivalent
to one mole of aluminum.

00:12:55.810 --> 00:13:01.169
Now we are interested in getting to the
final product, which is aluminum oxide.

00:13:01.599 --> 00:13:05.120
So we need to use our molar
coefficients to get there.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:10.340
So we see that four moles of
aluminum is equivalent to two moles.

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:17.390
Of AL 2 0 3, then we are going to
use the molar mass of aluminum oxide

00:13:17.719 --> 00:13:20.830
to get two grams as our final step.

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:31.650
So one mole of AL 2 0 3 is equivalent
to 101.957 grams a L 2 0 3.

00:13:32.859 --> 00:13:35.530
Before we continue, we want
to double check did all of

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:37.300
our units cancel properly?

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:42.650
So, we see grams are opposite each
other, they cancelled, moles cancelled

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:48.240
moles of aluminum, and then moles of
aluminum oxide cancelled moles of aluminum

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:51.030
oxide, leaving us only with the grams.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:56.749
So when we solve this,
we'll take 50 divided by 26.

00:13:56.750 --> 00:14:03.020
98 times by 2, Divide by
4, then multiply by 101.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:09.260
97, and what we will get for a
theoretical yield is about 94.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:12.779
5 grams of Al2O3.

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:19.179
This is our theoretical yield here,
which is what we've been doing all

00:14:19.179 --> 00:14:20.109
along when it comes to stoichiometry.

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:23.689
It's nothing new, it's just we're
just labeling it a little differently.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:26.000
Calculate the actual yield.

00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:26.880
How do we do it?

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:30.640
Well, the thing is, we
were given that we had 75%.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.870
percent yield in the problem.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:39.030
So what we are going to do is we're going
to pretty much take the problem and plug

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:42.300
in the theoretical yield that we got.

00:14:42.829 --> 00:14:47.520
So we have our actual yield and
then our theoretical was 94.

00:14:47.619 --> 00:14:49.720
5 grams of Al2O3.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:53.640
So the first thing we're going
to do is we're going to move the

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.170
100 percent to the other side.

00:14:55.505 --> 00:14:59.755
And what we'll quickly get is, alright,
75 divided by 100, we'll get 0.

00:14:59.795 --> 00:15:06.284
75, and that's gonna be equal to our
actual divided by our theoretical.

00:15:07.224 --> 00:15:10.344
And then we will multiply
both sides by 94.

00:15:10.374 --> 00:15:14.754
